  "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=1+3{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14163598/assets/198c3d6737439fcfa204926394b317aae0026502b5eaacb9e2f0429941f23db3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $1.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=1+3{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{7}{4}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A.\n$$",
